1
00:00:01,520 --> 00:00:05,010
Some questions have stayed
with us for a very long time.

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:07,650
How did the universe begin?

3
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:10,130
How did life arise?

4
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:14,890
What laws organize matter, space and time?

5
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:19,010
And what is our place
in this vast universe?

6
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:23,880
Science has taken us a long
way toward answering them.

7
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:26,880
We have looked deep into
the structure of matter,

8
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:29,200
read the information carried by life,

9
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:32,480
seen light that crossed the
universe over billions of years,

10
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:35,330
and sent our instruments beyond the Earth.

11
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:38,970
But every discovery opens new questions.

12
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:44,170
We tend to speak of exploring the
universe as if it were a world outside us.

13
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:47,730
But we have never stood outside nature.

14
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:53,170
The matter we are made of is
the matter of the universe.

15
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:58,250
The laws we try to understand are the
laws of the very world we live in.

16
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:01,720
Scientific knowledge is not
made in a single moment.

17
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:06,367
It is built over generations — from
observation, questions, experiments,

18
00:01:06,367 --> 00:01:11,040
models and data; from mistakes
recognized and understanding revised.

19
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:16,210
Each generation takes what came before,
tests it again, and adds something new.

20
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:21,050
VNISES was created in the hope of
contributing a part to that journey.

21
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:24,320
Here, science is not
only something to read.

22
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:26,890
A phenomenon can be observed.

23
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:29,730
A model can be changed.

24
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:32,770
An assumption can be tested.

25
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:35,930
A result can be compared.

26
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:40,090
And one question can lead to a deeper one.

27
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:43,680
Because science does not live
in separate compartments.

28
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:48,450
A question about light can lead to
quantum physics, and then to astronomy.

29
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:52,850
A problem of motion can lead
to orbits, and to spaceflight.

30
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:54,937
An observation of a galaxy can open

31
00:01:54,937 --> 00:01:58,210
questions about the history
of the entire universe.

32
00:01:58,560 --> 00:02:00,690
That is what Nexus means.

33
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:05,410
But discovery only has meaning
when it can be verified.

34
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:07,570
Data needs a source.

35
00:02:07,720 --> 00:02:10,490
Simulation must be
distinguished from observation.

36
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:12,730
Assumptions need to be identified.

37
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:15,650
A model's limits need to be shown.

38
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:20,370
And when the evidence is not yet enough,
the uncertainty must be stated clearly.

39
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,673
VNISES is not built to make
science look simpler than it is —

40
00:02:24,673 --> 00:02:27,050
nor to make it more complicated.

41
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:32,050
The aim is to make the path from
observation to understanding clearer.

42
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:38,010
There may always be questions
beyond what we currently understand.

43
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:42,290
And that is why the journey
of discovery continues.
